<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nerik</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nerik was a Hittite holy city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, sacred to the storm god of Nerik, usually identified with the mound of Oymaağaç near Vezirköprü. The Kaska overran it in the 15th century BCE, and for generations the Hittite kings could not hold it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> restored it in the 13th century BCE, and it remained an important cult centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nerik.jpg" alt="Nerik" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ingeborg Simon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oymaa%C4%9Fac_H%C3%B6y%C3%BCk_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nerik lay to the north of the Hittite capitals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> and Sapinuwa, probably in the Pontic region. Excavations from 2005 to 2009 led to its identification with Oymaağaç Höyük, on the eastern side of the Kızılırmak River, 7 km northwest of Vezirköprü. The site was occupied in the Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>. Its storm god, held to be the son of Wurušemu, the Sun-goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arinna">Arinna</a>, was associated with Mount Zaliyanu near the city, which was believed to bring it rain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nerik was founded by speakers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattic-language">Hattic language</a> under the name Narak, and a tablet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> archive, CTH 737, records a Hattic incantation for a festival there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> took the city under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a> and kept up a spring festival there, the Puruli, in honour of the Storm-god of Nerik, at which the myth of the slaying of the serpent Illuyanka was recited. The Hittites blamed the Kaska for the city's ruin, and the Puruli festival had to be moved to Hattusa; by the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-i">Tudhaliya I</a> the Kaska occupied the site.</p><p>In the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> his brother and governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> recaptured Nerik and rebuilt it as its High Priest, naming his firstborn son Nerikkaili after it, although he later passed him over for the succession. Seven years after Muwatalli's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urhi-teshub">Mursili III</a> became king, Mursili gave Nerik to another governor, and Hattusili rebelled and took the throne himself. Nerik disappears from the record with the fall of the Hittite kingdom, around 1200 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rainer Maria Czichon and Jörg Klinger of the Free University of Berlin began excavating Oymaağaç Höyük in 2005. It is the northernmost place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> with remains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>: fragments of tablets, a bulla with the stamps of the scribe Sarini, traces suggesting monumental architecture and mining tools for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> of nearby Tavşan Mountain. Cuneiform tablets found there point to the site as Nerik, and an inventory lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trays and golden bullae kept in a shrine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nerik” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They appear in Hittite texts from the 15th century BCE, when they overran the holy city of <b>Nerik</b> and pushed into central Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaska">Kaska</a></li></ul></section>
